    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let $x_1$ represent the number of ways in which person one's neighbors are seated on either side of person one. Similarly, let $x_2$ represent the number of ways in which person two's neighbors are situated. By the multiplication principle, we multiply together all the possible arrangements of each person.\nWe notice that each of these arrangements mirrors an arrangement we have already listed. Therefore, we used the multiplication principle twice since we noticed two choices for arrangements of a single person. In this way, we have performed $2 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 7$ multiplications each. Thus, there are $7 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 7 = \\boxed{343}$ ways to arrange the people.\nThe answer is: 343",
